{
  "image_name": "0f0bec0ead1bb0157bc3e455b70df437.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how intestinal and colonic epithelial organoids are generated and used. It shows that crypts or purified LGR5-positive stem cells can be isolated from the intestine and grown ex vivo in Matrigel with key niche factors such as Noggin, R-spondin1, EGF, and a Notch ligand. Under these conditions, the cells self-organize into hollow epithelial organoids with budding structures that resemble crypt and villus domains, capturing the architecture of the native tissue.\n\nA similar workflow is shown for the colon: cells from an LGR5-marked colonic crypt are dissociated, cultured, and expanded into RFP-positive colonic organoids. These donor organoids can then be transplanted into an injured colon in an orthotopic assay, where they appear to engraft into a DSS-induced wound bed and contribute to epithelial reconstruction.\n\nThe final part extends this approach to human tissue, starting from a colon biopsy, followed by crypt isolation and organoid culture. The resulting human organoids are presented as a versatile platform for basic research, diagnostic applications such as drug screening or mutation analysis, and potentially as a therapeutic resource for gastrointestinal disease.",
